From: microvm <microvm@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] x86/mm: don't poison freed kernel image pages unless init_on_free

free_init_pages() memsets every freed page of the kernel image (initmem,
the text/rodata and rodata/data 2 MB alignment gaps) with 0xcc. For a
microVM that is ~2.4 MB of guest memory the host must back for nothing:
the rodata/data gap isn't even part of the vmlinux file. Free those pages
without touching them unless init_on_free is on.

--- a/arch/x86/mm/init.c	2026-11-04 07:40:00.504273385 +0100
+++ b/arch/x86/mm/init.c	2026-11-04 07:40:00.524129888 +0100
@@ -934,8 +934,17 @@
 		set_memory_nx(begin, (end - begin) >> PAGE_SHIFT);
 		set_memory_rw(begin, (end - begin) >> PAGE_SHIFT);
 
+		/*
+		 * Poisoning writes every freed page: up to a few MB of
+		 * kernel image (init sections and the 2 MB alignment gaps)
+		 * that a guest would otherwise never touch, so a VMM never
+		 * has to back it. The pages are already NX and get the
+		 * usual init_on_alloc/init_on_free treatment on reuse;
+		 * keep the poison when init_on_free asks for scrubbing.
+		 */
 		free_reserved_area((void *)begin, (void *)end,
-				   POISON_FREE_INITMEM, what);
+				   want_init_on_free() ? POISON_FREE_INITMEM : -1,
+				   what);
 	}
 }
 
